The Cuisinart 14-Cup Programmable PerfecTemp (DCC-3200BKSNAS) makes a genuinely great cup of coffee. Past tense, because I woke up this morning to a dead one sitting on my counter.

This was my third Cuisinart in six years. Unit one, bought September 2019. Unit two, bought November 2021, replacing the first after it gave out. Unit three, bought November 2023 — the one that just died on me — replacing unit two two weeks before it would've hit its second birthday.

Unit three made it almost three years. Three months shy, to be exact. That's the best showing of the three, and it's still nothing to brag about for a machine that runs once, sometimes twice, a day, doing the one job it exists to do.

When it worked, it worked well — no complaints on performance while the thing was alive. That's not the problem. The problem is I keep waking up to a machine that quietly quit on me overnight.

I used to think of Cuisinart as a brand that cost more because it lasted longer. That math stopped working somewhere around unit two. Here's my actual gripe, and it's bigger than one coffee maker: this is planned obsolescence dressed up in stainless steel and a premium price tag. You're not paying for a machine built to last — you're paying for a machine built to look like it should. Somewhere along the way, "durable" and "expensive" quietly stopped being the same promise, and the profit margin on a coffee maker that dies in two years is a lot better for Cuisinart than the margin on one that actually makes it to year six.

My takeaway, for whatever it's worth to you: I'd rather buy something cheap that lasts exactly as long as something expensive, and pocket the difference. I'm done paying a brand-name tax for a countertop appliance that can't outlast its own warranty card by much.

Now shopping for whatever replaces it, over black coffee I had to make some other way this morning. Suggestions welcome.
